Perfect! Here's how the login information is built. There are two main versions:

LEAF_Nexus/templates/login.tpl
Smarty
{if $name == ''}
    <form name="login" method="post" action="?a=login">
    <font class="alert">STATUS: {$status}</font>
    <input name="login" type="submit" title="Click to login" value="Login" class="submit" />
    </form>
{else}
    Logged in as: <b>{$name}</b>
{/if}
LEAF_Request_Portal/admin/templates/login.tpl
Smarty
{if $name == ''}
    <form name="login" method="post" action="?a=login">
        <span class="alert">STATUS: {$status}</span>
        <input name="login" type="submit" title="Click to login" value="Login" class="submit" />
    </form>
{else}
    <ul class="leaf-user-menu" aria-haspopup="true">
        <li>Welcome, <span class="leaf-bold">{$name}</span><a href="../?a=logout">SIGN OUT</a></li>
    </ul>
{/if}
How it works:

The PHP files assign the $name variable to the login.tpl template before rendering it
The template checks if $name is empty:
If empty: displays a login form
If populated: displays the user's name (and in the admin version, a sign-out link)
The rendered output is then assigned to $login in the main template
Finally, $login is displayed in main.tpl with {$login|sanitize} (in LEAF_Nexus) or {$login} (in LEAF_Request_Portal)
The $name variable typically comes from the login object and contains the user's display name that was authenticated by the system.
